## Why This Question Is Asked
Tests candidates on practical lock-free allocator design with emphasis on per-core caching, ABA avoidance, and NUMA-aware padding. Also probes understanding of microbenchmarking for contention analysis.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free memory allocation and per-core caches
- ABA avoidance with tagged pointers or version counters
- Cache-line alignment and padding to prevent false sharing
- NUMA-aware data placement and cross-core contention

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct Block {
    struct Block* next;
    uintptr_t tag;
    unsigned char data[128];
} Block;

static _Atomic(Block*) g_free_list = NULL;

static Block* fetch_chunk(void) {
    Block* chunk = aligned_alloc(64, 256 * sizeof(Block));
    for (int i = 0; i < 255; i++) {
        chunk[i].next = &chunk[i + 1];
        chunk[i].tag = 0;
    }
    chunk[255].next = NULL;
    chunk[255].tag = 0;
    return chunk;
}

static Block* alloc_block(void) {
    Block* b = local_cache;  // thread-local
    do {
        if (b) return b;
    } while ((b = atomic_load_explicit(&g_free_list, memory_order_acquire)) 
             && !atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&g_free_list, &b, b->next));
    return b ? b : fetch_chunk();
}
```

## Microbenchmark Plan
1. **Baseline**: Measure allocation latency with warm local cache
2. **Contention test**: Scale threads from 8 to 32, measure global pool miss rate
3. **NUMA sensitivity**: Pin threads to cores on different sockets, measure cross-socket access overhead
4. **Metrics**: Track local-cache hits, global-pool fetches, CAS retry rate, and allocation/free latency percentiles

## Why This Question Is Asked

This question assesses the ability to design robust, low-latency IPC primitives under NUMA-aware multi-producer/multi-consumer contention with precise memory ordering requirements.

## Key Concepts

- Lock-free design, with ABA avoidance via per-slot sequence counters
- NUMA-aware memory placement to minimize cross-socket memory traffic
- Shadow consumer tail optimization to reduce remote cache-line access
- Cache-line alignment to prevent false sharing
- Memory ordering semantics (acquire/release, memory_order_acq_rel)

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
    volatile uint64_t sequence;
    char data[56];
} slot_t;

typedef struct {
    slot_t slots[N] __attribute__((aligned(64)));
    volatile uint64_t head;
    volatile uint64_t tail;
    volatile uint64_t shadow_tail;
} mpmc_queue_t;

static inline int enqueue(mpmc_queue_t* q, const void* data) {
    uint64_t slot = __atomic_fetch_add(&q->tail, 1, __ATOMIC_ACQ_REL);
    slot_t* s = &q->slots[slot & (N - 1)];
    while (s->sequence != slot) { /* spin */ }
    memcpy(s->data, data, 56);
    __atomic_store(&s->sequence, slot + 1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
    return 0;
}

static inline int dequeue(mpmc_queue_t* q, void* data) {
    uint64_t slot = __atomic_fetch_add(&q->head, 1, __ATOMIC_ACQ_REL);
    slot_t* s = &q->slots[slot & (N - 1)];
    while (s->sequence != slot + 1) { /* spin */ }
    memcpy(data, s->data, 56);
    __atomic_store(&s->sequence, slot + N, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
    return 0;
}
```

## Microbenchmark Outline

To isolate NUMA effects under asymmetric producer/consumer loads:

1. **Setup**: Run producers on socket 0, consumers on socket 1; measure round-trip latency and throughput.
2. **Workload injection**: Vary producer/consumer ratios (e.g., 10:1, 1:10, 5:5) to create asymmetric contention.
3. **Metrics**: Capture remote memory accesses via `numactl --hardware`, cache-miss ratios via `perf stat`, and per-socket latency distributions.
4. **Validation**: Compare interleaved vs. local-only memory placement to quantify NUMA effects; verify shadow tail reduces remote cache-line bouncing.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a safe, efficient memory primitive from scratch, focusing on low-level concurrency and memory layout rather than library calls.

## Key Concepts

- Fixed-size allocators and arena preallocation
- Free-list embedded in blocks
- Lock-free allocation/free with atomic CAS
- Memory alignment and fragmentation basics
- Basic multi-threaded testing strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Sketch in C-like pseudocode
#include <stdatomic.h>
typedef struct Block { struct Block* next; unsigned char data[64]; } Block;
static unsigned char arena[64*1024];
_Atomic(Block*) head;
void init_pool() { head = NULL; /* link blocks in arena */ }
void* alloc_block() { Block* b; while ((b = atomic_load(&head)) && !atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&head, (Block**)&head, (Block*)b->next)); return b ? b->data : NULL; }
void free_block(void* p) { Block* b = (Block*)((char*)p - offsetof(Block, data)); b->next = atomic_load(&head); while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&head, (Block**)&head, b)); }
```

